ize.\n- Layers are painted largest-first, so inner layers end up on top of outer ones.\n\n**Working the layer offsets by hand (not yet run on the grids)**\n\n- **Pair 1** (template box rows 2–11, cols 3–13):\n  - The 6-layer has offsets 3/0/3/3. In the 9×9 target at rows 8–16, cols 19–27, that gives rows 11–13, cols 19–24.\n  - The 8-layer has offsets 4/0/4/4, giving row 12, cols 19–23.\n  - The large target gives 6 at rows 22–25, cols 5–17 and 8 at rows 23–24, cols 5–16.\n  - These cells match the expected output.\n- **Pair 2**: the offsets are 2/2/0/2 for 4, 4/4/0/4 for 1, and 6/6/0/6 for 8. In the target they give 4 at rows 17–23, cols 16–26, then 1 at rows 19–23, cols 18–24, then 8 at rows 21–23, cols 20–22. This matches.\n- **Pair 3**: 4 is 2 in from every edge and 2 is 3 in from every edge. This reproduces both the small target and the big one.\n\n**Test**\n\n- The template is the vertical-stripe rectangle at rows 1–10, cols 2–14.\n- Its layers have top/bottom offset 0. Their left/right offsets are 2 for colour 2, 4 for colour 8, and 5 for colour 4. Colours 2 and 8 each appear as two stripes, but each colour's bounding box spans across, and the inner layers paint over the middle.\n- These layers are stamped into both plain 1-rectangles: rows 14–25 × cols 2–15, and rows 3–17 × cols 18–28.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    import collections\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True)\n    tmpl = None\n    for o in objs:\n        cols = set(int(g[y, x]) for y, x in o[\"cells\"])\n        if len(cols) > 1:\n            tmpl = o\n            break\n    if tmpl is None:\n        emit(out, \"No patterned template found; nothing to copy.\")\n        return out\n    targets = [o for o in objs if o is not tmpl]\n    if not targets:\n        emit(out, \"No plain rectangles to decorate.\")\n        return out\n    base = collections.Counter(int(g[o[\"cells\"][0]]) for o in targets).most_common(1)[0][0]\n    ty0, tx0, ty1, tx1 = tmpl[\"box\"]\n    layers = []\n    tcells = collections.defaultdict(list)\n    for y, x in tmpl[\"cells\"]:\n        c = int(g[y, x])\n        if c != base:\n            tcells[c].append((y, x))\n    for c, cells in tcells.items():\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        area = (y1 - y0 + 1) * (x1 - x0 + 1)\n        layers.append((area, c, y0 - ty0, x0 - tx0, ty1 - y1, tx1 - x1))\n    layers.sort(key=lambda t: -t[0])\n    emit(out, \"Template found; its layers keep fixed distances from the rectangle edges.\")\n    for o in targets:\n        if int(g[o[\"cells\"][0]]) != base:\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        for _, c, dt, dl, db, dr in layers:\n            a, b, cc, d = y0 + dt, x0 + dl, y1 - db, x1 - dr\n            if a <= cc and b <= d:\n                out[a:cc + 1, b:d + 1] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Stamped the layered pattern into the rectangle at {o['box']}, keeping each layer's edge offsets.\")\n    emit(out, \"All plain rectangles now carry the template pattern.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Layers are painted largest-first, so inner layers end up on top of outer ones."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Exactly one rectangle (the template) contains a nested pattern of colours."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It sits at fixed distances (top, left, bottom, right) from the edges of the template."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1** (template box rows 2–11, cols 3–13):"}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Layers are painted largest-first, so inner layers end up on top of outer ones."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Colours 2 and 8 each appear as two stripes, but each colour's bounding box spans across, and the inner layers paint over the middle."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- To decorate a plain rectangle, paint each layer at those same edge distances."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Layers are painted largest-first, so inner layers end up on top of outer ones."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Working the layer offsets by hand (not yet run on the grids)**"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"708289ac4dcbcb03cd0196faae82a72a4be7c72e4033e23de3f08bf135bd50d3","task_sha256":"2c618513396e747f906b8fd539cd95145d010631d1626ec6bda3c4b6b440b46c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
